Carafe: with d3d9.effectThread on top of d3d9.frontEndThread, the effect library a game links into its executable runs
on a thread of its own. The device gets a second front end in front of the first: the game thread queues its device
calls and the effect methods it calls, a worker "d3dx-effects" runs them, so the effect library's state work and its
device calls happen there, and that worker feeds the "dxvk-frontend" queue, which keeps the shadow and drops unchanged
states. The effect interface is found as for the counters, must have the 86-method layout, and each queued method must
pop the argument bytes its signature has, or nothing changes; parameter setters copy their data into the queue,
Begin answers the pass count it got for the same technique before, and every other public method, Release included,
first waits for the queue. Each front end has its own buffer staging; a gate waits for both layers except on their
own workers; the effect worker takes the game thread's x87 control word.
Zlib, as the files it changes.

